---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Sopsdeck — a visual browser and editor for your secrets"
  description="A visual browser and editor for your secrets. Manage SOPS-encrypted dotenv, JSON, and YAML files locally, on your own or with a team."
  active="product"
>
  <p class="dev-banner" data-testid="site-dev-banner">
    Sopsdeck is under development. Expect rough edges.
    <a href="https://github.com/sopsdeck/sopsdeck/issues/new">Submit a GitHub issue</a>
  </p>
  <section class="hero hero--landing">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <div class="kicker">For individual developers and teams</div>
      <h1>A visual browser and editor for your secrets.</h1>
      <p>
        A simple, flexible way to manage SOPS-encrypted dotenv, JSON, and YAML files. Edit locally,
        share access with your team, and track changes in Git.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="#start">Install with npm</a>
        <a class="button ghost" href="#features">See how it works</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-still">
      <img
        src="/assets/editor.png"
        width="1440"
        height="900"
        alt="Sopsdeck showing a production secrets file with values hidden"
      />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="features" id="features">
    <div class="kicker">Features</div>
    <h2>What you can do</h2>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <a class="card card-link" href="/docs/guide.html#rename-keys">
        <div class="kicker">Rename</div>
        <h3>Update references when keys change</h3>
        <p>
          Rename a secret and rewrite <code>KEY</code>, <code>$KEY</code>, and
          <code>${'${KEY}'}</code> across the Project so application code keeps matching.
        </p>
      </a>
      <a class="card card-link" href="/docs/guide.html#lock-files">
        <div class="kicker">Disk</div>
        <h3>Lock and unlock files</h3>
        <p>
          Unlock writes plaintext for local tools. Lock encrypts again. The editor badge follows
          that state.
        </p>
      </a>
      <a class="card card-link" href="/docs/guide.html#unused-secrets">
        <div class="kicker">Scan</div>
        <h3>Unused secret detection</h3>
        <p>
          Keys with zero references in the Project show an unused badge so you can clean up
          leftovers.
        </p>
      </a>
      <a class="card card-link" href="/docs/guide.html#secret-history">
        <div class="kicker">Git</div>
        <h3>View each version of a secret or file</h3>
        <p>
          Browse file history and a single key’s revisions. Restore a revision without committing
          it.
        </p>
      </a>
      <a class="card card-link" href="/docs/guide.html#field-encryption">
        <div class="kicker">JSON / YAML</div>
        <h3>Encrypt specific fields</h3>
        <p>
          Encrypt only the leaves you choose in <code>package.json</code>, <code>eas.json</code>, or
          Compose YAML. The rest stays plaintext.
        </p>
      </a>
      <a class="card card-link" href="/docs/guide.html#access">
        <div class="kicker">Access</div>
        <h3>Share access with your team</h3>
        <p>
          Add a teammate’s Age public key, or copy a request that includes yours. Project owners
          approve new people.
        </p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split" id="encrypted-in-place">
    <div>
      <div class="kicker">SOPS</div>
      <h2>Keys stay readable. Values encrypt in place.</h2>
      <p>
        A locked file is still JSON, YAML, or dotenv. Unencrypted fields and key names are
        plaintext. Only the values you selected become <code>ENC[…]</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/docs/guide.html#encrypted-in-place">See how a locked file looks</a>
      </p>
    </div>
    <pre
      class="encrypted-snippet"
      aria-label="Locked eas.json example"><code>{`{
  "cli": { "version": "20.5.1" },
  "build": {
    "env": {
      "EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL": "https://api.example.com",
      "EXPO_TOKEN": "ENC[AES256_GCM,data:m4nY8pQ=,type:str]"
    }
  },
  "sops": {
    "age": [{ "recipient": "age1…" }]
  }
}`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="start" id="start">
    <div class="kicker">Install · macOS, Windows, and Linux</div>
    <h2>Open Sopsdeck in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      Install with npm, then open a project folder. SOPS is included; all you need is Node.js and
      Git.
    </p>
    <pre>npm install -D @sopsdeck/sopsdeck
npx sopsdeck .

# global shortcut
npm install -g @sopsdeck/sopsdeck
sd .</pre>
    <p>The npm package downloads the matching local runner on first launch.</p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
